Building MoCoLinux end to end
=============================

From a clean checkout to the zip that gets published. `doc/building-modern`
covers the Windows daemons and driver in detail and `doc/building-copresent`
covers the GL stack; this is the ordering that ties them together, the
prerequisites each step actually needs, and the traps that have cost real time.

Everything here runs on a Linux build host. Nothing is built on Windows.


0. What is built, and by what
-----------------------------

Six independent products end up in a release. Knowing which script owns which
matters, because rebuilding the wrong one is the usual reason a fix does not
appear on the test box.

    product                     built by                     lands in
    ------------------------    -------------------------    ----------------
    driver + daemons            tools/build.sh               dist-x64/
      linux.sys, colinux-daemon.exe, colinux-slirp-net-daemon.exe,
      cogpu-daemon.exe, coxwire, and the installer
    renderer DLLs               meson, by hand (step 2)      download/prefix-mingw/bin
      libvirglrenderer-1.dll, libepoxy-0.dll
    guest kernel                the 7.1.5 patch + make       dist-x64/vmlinux
    guest GL stack              tools/build-mesa-copresent.sh
      libGL/libEGL/libGLESv2/gallium/libgbm, BOTH ABIs       build/mesa-copresent-26.1.6/out/{lib64,lib32}
    guest root filesystem       tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh     an image file
    the release directory       tools/mkrelease.sh VERSION   release/MoCoLinux-VERSION

The X server is not built. VcXsrv 1.14.2.1 ships as its stock installer and is
never rebuilt -- the 2013 MSVC toolchain it needs is not something we have.

Its binary is patched after installation, once, by
tools/vcxsrv-fakemonitor/patch-vcxsrv.py: stock VcXsrv reports no monitor at
all, and clients that enumerate displays before opening a window fail on that.
The patch refuses to touch anything but the exact 1.14.2.1 build it was written
against, so a reinstall silently reverts it and it has to be reapplied.


1. Build host prerequisites
---------------------------

A current Arch-derived distribution is the accepted host; CachyOS is what these
were verified on. Per step:

  * Windows side: `mingw-w64-gcc`, `python3`, `osslsigncode`.
  * Renderer DLLs: `meson`, `ninja`, and a Python with **PyYAML** — see the
    trap in step 2.
  * Guest GL stack: a C/C++ toolchain, `python3` with venv support, `ninja`,
    `patch`, `pkg-config`, and the development files for X11/XCB, libdrm,
    expat, zlib, zstd, sensors and SPIR-V Tools.
  * The 32-bit half of the GL stack additionally needs `gcc -m32` (multilib)
    and the i686 development libraries for that same set. On Arch those are the
    `lib32-*` packages. Without them the script skips the 32-bit half and says
    so — see the trap in step 4.
  * Root filesystem: `pacman` and `e2fsprogs`.
  * Release: `bsdtar` (there is no `zip` on the accepted host), and `gh` to
    publish.


2. The renderer DLLs
--------------------

Build these first: `cogpu-daemon` includes `<virgl/virglrenderer.h>` and
`<epoxy/gl.h>` from `download/prefix-mingw`, so the Windows build fails without
them.

virglrenderer is the pristine 1.3.0 release tarball plus the patches in
`download/virgl-patches/`, applied in order:

    01-drm-h-windows-no-ioccom.patch   supplies the fixed-width types and
                                       collapses the ioctl encoders, because
                                       Windows has no <sys/ioccom.h> and this
                                       side never issues a DRM ioctl
    02-moco-virgl.patch                the MoCoLinux delta: the
                                       VIRGL_CCMD_MOCO_PRESENT command and its
                                       decoder, the v5 present callback, and
                                       two shader-emission fixes

Then, from `download/virglrenderer-1.3.0`:

    meson setup build-clean \
        -Dprefix=<repo>/download/prefix-mingw \
        -Dplatforms= -Dtests=false -Dvenus=false \
        -Dminigbm_allocation=false \
        --cross-file=<repo>/download/mingw64-cross.ini
    ninja -C build-clean
    ninja -C build-clean install

**Trap: meson needs PyYAML, and the error does not say so usefully.** It fails
with "python3 is missing modules: yaml". If the system Python lacks it, create a
virtual environment with `meson` and `PyYAML` in it and put that environment's
`bin` first on `PATH` for the whole invocation — meson re-executes Python for
generated sources, so exporting `PYTHONPATH` alone is not enough.

**Trap: bumping the callback ABI silently disables the GPU.** `vrend_if_cbs`
and `virgl_renderer_callbacks` are versioned, and
`virgl_renderer_init` rejects a callbacks struct whose `version` exceeds
`VIRGL_RENDERER_CALLBACKS_VERSION`. Raising the version in cogpu without raising
that constant makes the daemon log "virglrenderer would not initialise -- no GPU
today" and exit, after which the guest hangs on its first GPU command because
nothing services the virtio queue. Change both, and rebuild both.


3. The Windows side
-------------------

    tools/build.sh

That is the whole recipe. It runs `bin/make.py`, signs the driver, builds the
installer, and stages everything into `../dist-x64`. Section 3 of
`doc/building-modern` explains what it does and how to build pieces of it by
hand for compiler diagnostics; note that a raw build directory is not an
install.

**Trap: never pipe this through `tail` or `head`.** The exit status then comes
from the pager, so a failed build reports success — and if it is followed by a
deploy in the same `&&` chain, the previous binary ships instead. This has
happened; run it bare and read the output.


4. The guest GL stack
---------------------

    MOCO_BUILD_JOBS=16 tools/build-mesa-copresent.sh

Verifies the official Mesa 26.1.6 tarball checksum, applies
`patch/mesa-26.1.6/moco-copresent.diff` with zero fuzz, and builds
`libGL`, `libEGL`, `libGLESv2`, the Gallium DRI library and `libgbm` — twice,
once native and once for i686 via `tools/i686-cross.ini`.

Both ABIs matter. Steam's client is i386 and asks the 32-bit loader for GL and
Vulkan; with a 64-bit-only stack it renders in software and its Vulkan probe
fails with `VK_ERROR_INCOMPATIBLE_DRIVER`. The script skips rather than fails
when the host has no multilib toolchain, so **read its output** — "skipping the
32-bit stack" means the release will be half a stack.

**Trap: the 32-bit configure needs `-Dxlib-lease=disabled`.** The script passes
it. It is a Vulkan display-lease feature requiring 32-bit libXrandr, which
multilib installs frequently lack, and no Vulkan driver is built here anyway.

**Trap: the i686 pkg-config path needs two directories.** `/usr/lib32/pkgconfig`
alone fails with "Package xproto was not found" — the header-only X protocol
packages exist once, in `/usr/share/pkgconfig`, for both ABIs. The cross file
sets both.

Copy the two output directories to `../dist-guest/lib64` and
`../dist-guest/lib32`, which is where the root filesystem builder looks for
them.


5. Guest binaries that run in the guest
---------------------------------------

    tools/build-copresent-guest.sh    the standalone present test producer
    tools/glbench/build.sh            the benchmark, built for WGL and GLX

**Trap: strip `.note.gnu.property` from anything built for the guest.** A build
host defaulting to x86-64-v3 (CachyOS does) stamps its own ISA level onto the
CRT objects even when the source is compiled `-march=x86-64`, and the guest
loader then refuses the binary before `main()` with "CPU ISA level is lower than
required". `objcopy --remove-section=.note.gnu.property` is the fix; the
existing scripts already do it, and anything new must too.


6. The guest root filesystem
----------------------------

    tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh <image-or-device> [size]

Needs `pacman` and `e2fsprogs`, and downloads about 2.5 GB. It installs the
CoPresent GL stack for both ABIs from `../dist-guest`, adds the ldconfig
directory that selects it, installs `vulkan-swrast` for both ABIs, and bakes
itself plus `coxwire` into the image so the installed system can build the next
one.

Read its warnings. "no CoPresent Mesa found -- GL will be software only" means
step 4 did not land where it looks, and the resulting image will boot fine and
render everything on the CPU.

**The shipped `root-arch.img` is a seed, not a desktop.** It carries this
script and a first-boot unit; the guest builds its real root filesystem onto a
second disk on first boot. So a guest-side change ships by going *into the
seed*, where it propagates.

Updating the seed does not mean rebuilding it. Mounting needs root; use
`debugfs`, which writes into the ext filesystem directly and unprivileged:

    debugfs -w -f commands.debugfs root-arch.img
    e2fsck -fy root-arch.img

Useful commands inside that file are `cd`, `rm`, `write <hostfile> <name>`,
`mkdir`, `symlink <link> <target>` and `sif <name> mode 0100755`. `dumpe2fs -h`
shows the free space. **Verify by dumping the files back out** with
`debugfs -R "dump <path> <out>"` and `cmp` — a failed `write` is otherwise
silent.


7. Cutting a release
--------------------

Bump `MOCO_VERSION` in `installer/setup.c`, rebuild so the installer carries it,
then:

    tools/mkrelease.sh 0.6.1

The manifest is not written by that script — it is read out of `setup.c`'s
payload array, so a file the installer expects and the tree does not have stops
the build instead of the install. It also refuses to proceed when the driver is
unsigned or was modified after signing, when the installer is older than
`setup.c`, when a launcher in the release differs from the tracked one, or when
any shipped binary links the Universal CRT or depends on a DLL the release does
not carry.

Then package and publish:

    cd release
    bsdtar -a -c -f MoCoLinux-0.6.1.zip MoCoLinux-0.6.1
    gh release create v0.6.1 MoCoLinux-0.6.1.zip \
        --title "MoCoLinux 0.6.1" --notes-file <notes> --target mocolinux

**Before publishing, check the daemon you are shipping is the one you tested.**
`sha256sum` the release copy against `dist-x64` and against the file installed
on the test box. They should be identical; when they are not, something was
rebuilt after the measurement and the numbers in the release notes describe a
binary nobody will download.


8. Order of operations
----------------------

For a clean tree, the dependencies force this order:

    1. renderer DLLs        (the Windows build includes their headers)
    2. tools/build.sh       (driver, daemons, installer, staging)
    3. guest GL stack       (both ABIs, into ../dist-guest)
    4. guest binaries       (test producer, glbench)
    5. root filesystem      (consumes ../dist-guest)
    6. tools/mkrelease.sh   (consumes dist-x64 and the image)

Steps 3 to 5 are only needed when the guest side changed. A host-only fix — the
common case for a hotfix — is steps 2 and 6, reusing the existing image, which
is why `cogpu-daemon.exe` can be the only file that differs between two
releases.
